'''Rongotai''' is a [[New Zealand electorates|New Zealand electorate]], returning a single member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for Rongotai is [[Julie Anne Genter]] of the [[New Zealand Green Party|Green Party]]. She has held this position since the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]].

The electorate will be abolished ahead of the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].

==Population centres==
The Rongotai electorate is centred on the southern and eastern suburbs of [[Wellington]] City. It stretches from [[Miramar, New Zealand|Miramar]] in the east to take in the suburbs of [[Rongotai]], [[Kilbirnie, New Zealand|Kilbirnie]], [[Lyall Bay]] and [[Hataitai]] and runs from the south coast at [[Island Bay]] up through the southern section of the [[Brooklyn, Wellington|Brooklyn]] Hill to an east–west border next to [[Wellington Hospital, New Zealand|Wellington Hospital]] in [[Newtown, New Zealand|Newtown]]. Because [[Wellington International Airport|Wellington Airport]] is within Rongotai's boundaries, the constituency also contains the [[Chatham Islands]]. It is named after the suburb of Rongotai which is roughly in its centre. Other suburbs include [[Berhampore, New Zealand|Berhampore]], [[Ōwhiro Bay]], [[Seatoun]], and [[Roseneath, Wellington|Roseneath]].

A revision after 1996 pulled the boundary southwards, moving the suburbs around the [[Basin Reserve]] and the [[Massey University]] campus into the {{NZ electorate link|Wellington Central}} electorate.{{citation needed|date=October 2014}} In the 2002 redistribution, the area covered by the Rongotai electorate did not change.<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2002 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2002%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=0-478-20169-9 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |format=PDF |date=21 March 2002}}</ref> Changes to boundaries were done in the 2007 redistribution,<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=11 |format=PDF |date=14 September 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Farrar |first1=David |author-link1=David Farrar (blogger) |title=Proposed 2008 Boundaries |url= http://www.kiwiblog.co.nz/2007/05/proposed_2008_boundaries.html |publisher=[[Kiwiblog]] |access-date=3 October 2014 |quote=Note that what is discussed in this entry was what the Representation Commission put out for public consultation; this does not represent the final decision.}}</ref> but no further changes were done in the 2013/14<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |format=PDF |date=4 April 2014}}</ref> or 2019/20 redistributions.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020 |access-date= 18 April 2020}}</ref>

==History==
Rongotai was one of the original 65 [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) representation electorates drawn in 1994 ahead of the [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996 election]]. It is the successor to the old {{NZ electorate link|Island Bay}} and {{NZ electorate link|Miramar}} electorates, though the areas in these seats in the orbit of the central city were incorporated into a redrawn [[Wellington Central (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington Central electorate]].

[[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]]'s [[Annette King]] was elected and re-elected as the member of parliament for Rongotai at all seven elections from 1996 to {{NZ election link year|2014}}.<ref name="Parliamentary profile current">{{cite web |title=Hon Annette King |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current/51MP581/king-hon-annette |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=7 October 2014 |date=22 September 2014}}</ref> In five out of the seven elections, Labour also won the party vote; the exception being in 1996 when [[New Zealand National Party|National]] out-polled Labour by just 68 votes,<ref name="1996 electorate votes">{{cite web |title=Part V – Electorate Summary of Votes for Registered Parties |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/4.1%20Votes%20for%20Registered%20Parties%20by%20Electorate.pdf |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=9 October 2014 |format=PDF}}</ref> and in 2014, when National's majority was 852 votes.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-46.html |title=Official Count Results – Rongotai |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=10 October 2014 |date=4 October 2014}}</ref> [[Chris Finlayson]] of the National Party opposed King, his distant cousin, since the {{NZ election link|2008}}. After the 2014 election, he told his supporters that on current trends, he should be able to win the electorate by 2038.<ref name="Diary Australia">{{cite journal |last1=Finlayson |first1=Chris |author-link1=Chris Finlayson |title=Diary Australia |journal=[[The Spectator]] |date=27 September 2014 }}</ref>

King announced in March 2017 that she was stepping down from her role as Labour's deputy leader and would retire from politics at the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]].<ref>{{cite news |url =http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=11809793 |access-date=27 September 2017|title=Claire Trevett on Annette King's resignation: 'Labour has lost one of its giants'|date=1 March 2017 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]}}</ref> The electorate of Rongotai was won in the election by [[Paul Eagle]], retaining it for Labour.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 September 2017 |title=Rongotai – Preliminary Count |url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electorate-details-46.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]|access-date=24 September 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200117212835/http://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/electorate-details-46.html|archive-date=17 January 2020|url-status=live}}</ref>

During the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]], Eagle retained Rongotai for Labour based on preliminary results.<ref>{{cite web |title=Rongotai – Preliminary Count |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020_preliminary/electorate-details-43.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=31 October 2020 }}</ref> Eagle announced in June 2022 that he would contest the [[2022 Wellington City mayoral election]] and should he be successful, he would resign from Parliament and trigger a by-election.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/rongotai-mp-paul-eagle-announces-wellington-mayoral-bid/ZD5WUG3IOJLSLDWMFZVR3UCJ74/ |title=Rongotai MP Paul Eagle announces Wellington mayoral bid |date=27 June 2022 |access-date=27 July 2022 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Georgina |last=Campbell}}</ref>

The 2025 boundary review required a reduction in North Island electorates by one to reflect nationwide population shifts. The three Wellington City electorates (Rongotai, [[Wellington Central (electorate)|Wellington Central]] and [[Ōhāriu (electorate)|Ōhāriu]]) were all underpopulated. All were abolished. The suburbs of [[Mount Cook, Wellington|Mount Cook]] and [[Brooklyn, Wellington|Brooklyn]] from Wellington Central were combined with the former Rongotai electorate and the new electorate was named [[Wellington Bays (electorate)|Wellington Bays]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Natanahira|first=Tuwhenuaroa|last2=Online|first2=Māori news journalist in Parliament RNZ|date=8 August 2025|title=From new names to new boundaries: Here's what's happening to your voting electorate|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/569410/from-new-names-to-new-boundaries-here-s-what-s-happening-to-your-voting-electorate|access-date=22 August 2025|website=RNZ|language=en-nz}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025|url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf|isbn=978-0-473-75233-0|publisher=Representation Commission|access-date=16 September 2025|page=18|date=8 August 2025}}</ref>

